# Pagination of container listings (LWS 1.0 core section 12.1.2).
"@context":
  - ../context.jsonld
  - ../touchstone.jsonld
id: "#manifest"
type: Manifest
label: "LWS 1.0 core: pagination"
comment: >-
  The threshold above which a server paginates is its own choice, and the format can
  neither read a page size from the target nor create members in bulk. So each test
  builds a container with five members. The multi-page tests apply only when the server
  splits it into pages (its first page carries rel="next"); pagination-single-page covers
  a server that presents the five as one paginated page. A server that does neither is
  inapplicable here, not failed (D-0062). Page URIs are opaque: every page is reached
  through a link the server gave.
specification:
  - https://www.w3.org/TR/2026/WD-lws10-core-20260921/
entries:

  - id: "#pagination-first-page"
    type: ValidationTest
    name: pagination-first-page
    label: The first page of a container split into pages has rel="first" and no rel="prev"
    comment: >-
      Requesting the container's own URI yields the first page, with 200. rel="first" MUST
      be present on a paginated response and rel="prev" MUST be omitted on the first page.
      The body is the container's: its id and type reflect the whole container.
    status: Proposed
    level: MUST
    source:
      - https://www.w3.org/TR/2026/WD-lws10-core-20260921/#pagination-link-relations
      - https://www.w3.org/TR/2026/WD-lws10-core-20260921/#requesting-pages
      - https://www.w3.org/TR/2026/WD-lws10-core-20260921/#pagination-model
    traits: [Get, Container]
    requirements:
      - https://example.org/touchstone/req/lws10-core/pagination-link-first
      - https://example.org/touchstone/req/lws10-core/pagination-link-prev
      - https://example.org/touchstone/req/lws10-core/pagination-200-totalitems-all-pages
      - https://example.org/touchstone/req/lws10-core/pagination-first-page-flow
    prereqs:
      hierarchy:
        - container: paged
        - dataResource: m1
          in: paged
          contentType: text/plain
          body: member 1
        - dataResource: m2
          in: paged
          contentType: text/plain
          body: member 2
        - dataResource: m3
          in: paged
          contentType: text/plain
          body: member 3
        - dataResource: m4
          in: paged
          contentType: text/plain
          body: member 4
        - dataResource: m5
          in: paged
          contentType: text/plain
          body: member 5
    steps:
      - label: the server splits a five-member container into pages (otherwise this test does not apply)
        precondition: true
        request:
          method: GET
          url: "${paged}"
          accept: application/lws+json
        response:
          statusCode: 200
          linkHeaders:
            - rel: first
            - rel: next
      - label: the first page does not link back
        request:
          method: GET
          url: "${paged}"
          accept: application/lws+json
        response:
          statusCode: 200
          contentType: application/lws+json
          linkHeaders:
            - rel: first
            - rel: prev
              absent: true
          json:
            - pointer: /id
              equalsIri: "${paged}"
            - pointer: /type
              hasValue: Container
            - pointer: /items
              jsonType: array

  - id: "#pagination-next-page"
    type: ValidationTest
    name: pagination-next-page
    label: Following rel="next" yields a further page of the same container, without the first page's members
    comment: >-
      The next page is reached only through the URI the server gave. It is 200, still
      carries rel="first", describes the same container, and does not repeat the first
      member of page one.
    status: Proposed
    level: MUST
    source:
      - https://www.w3.org/TR/2026/WD-lws10-core-20260921/#pagination-link-relations
      - https://www.w3.org/TR/2026/WD-lws10-core-20260921/#requesting-pages
      - https://www.w3.org/TR/2026/WD-lws10-core-20260921/#pagination-model
    traits: [Get, Container]
    requirements:
      - https://example.org/touchstone/req/lws10-core/pagination-link-first
      - https://example.org/touchstone/req/lws10-core/pagination-200-totalitems-all-pages
    prereqs:
      hierarchy:
        - container: paged
        - dataResource: m1
          in: paged
          contentType: text/plain
          body: member 1
        - dataResource: m2
          in: paged
          contentType: text/plain
          body: member 2
        - dataResource: m3
          in: paged
          contentType: text/plain
          body: member 3
        - dataResource: m4
          in: paged
          contentType: text/plain
          body: member 4
        - dataResource: m5
          in: paged
          contentType: text/plain
          body: member 5
    steps:
      - label: the server splits a five-member container into pages (otherwise this test does not apply)
        precondition: true
        request:
          method: GET
          url: "${paged}"
          accept: application/lws+json
        response:
          statusCode: 200
          linkHeaders:
            - rel: first
            - rel: next
      - label: read the first page, keeping its next link and its first member
        request:
          method: GET
          url: "${paged}"
          accept: application/lws+json
        response:
          statusCode: 200
          linkHeaders:
            - rel: next
              capture: next
          json:
            - pointer: /items/0/id
              capture: firstMember
      - label: the next page is another page of the same container
        request:
          method: GET
          url: "${next}"
          accept: application/lws+json
        response:
          statusCode: 200
          linkHeaders:
            - rel: first
          json:
            - pointer: /id
              equalsIri: "${paged}"
            - pointer: /items
              jsonType: array
            - pointer: /items
              none:
                - pointer: /id
                  equalsIri: "${firstMember}"

  - id: "#pagination-single-page"
    type: ValidationTest
    name: pagination-single-page
    label: A paginated listing that holds every member on one page has neither rel="next" nor rel="prev"
    comment: >-
      A server may present a container as paginated (rel="first") even when all its
      members fit on one page. That page is both the first and the last, so rel="prev"
      ("MUST be omitted on the first page") and rel="next" ("MUST be omitted on the last
      page") are both absent. Applies when the five-member container's paginated listing
      carries all five.
    status: Proposed
    level: MUST
    source:
      - https://www.w3.org/TR/2026/WD-lws10-core-20260921/#pagination-link-relations
    traits: [Get, Container]
    requirements:
      - https://example.org/touchstone/req/lws10-core/pagination-link-next
      - https://example.org/touchstone/req/lws10-core/pagination-link-prev
      - https://example.org/touchstone/req/lws10-core/pagination-link-first
    prereqs:
      hierarchy:
        - container: paged
        - dataResource: m1
          in: paged
          contentType: text/plain
          body: member 1
        - dataResource: m2
          in: paged
          contentType: text/plain
          body: member 2
        - dataResource: m3
          in: paged
          contentType: text/plain
          body: member 3
        - dataResource: m4
          in: paged
          contentType: text/plain
          body: member 4
        - dataResource: m5
          in: paged
          contentType: text/plain
          body: member 5
    steps:
      - label: the listing is paginated and holds all five members on its page (otherwise this test does not apply)
        precondition: true
        request:
          method: GET
          url: "${paged}"
          accept: application/lws+json
        response:
          statusCode: 200
          linkHeaders:
            - rel: first
          json:
            - pointer: /items/4
              exists: true
      - label: the one page links neither forward nor back
        request:
          method: GET
          url: "${paged}"
          accept: application/lws+json
        response:
          statusCode: 200
          linkHeaders:
            - rel: next
              absent: true
            - rel: prev
              absent: true

  - id: "#pagination-last-page"
    type: ValidationTest
    name: pagination-last-page
    label: The last page has rel="first" and no rel="next"
    comment: >-
      rel="last" is a MAY, so the test applies only when the first page offers it. The
      last page is 200, keeps rel="first", and omits rel="next", which "MUST be omitted on
      the last page".
    status: Proposed
    level: MUST
    source:
      - https://www.w3.org/TR/2026/WD-lws10-core-20260921/#pagination-link-relations
    traits: [Get, Container]
    requirements:
      - https://example.org/touchstone/req/lws10-core/pagination-link-last
      - https://example.org/touchstone/req/lws10-core/pagination-link-next
      - https://example.org/touchstone/req/lws10-core/pagination-link-first
    prereqs:
      hierarchy:
        - container: paged
        - dataResource: m1
          in: paged
          contentType: text/plain
          body: member 1
        - dataResource: m2
          in: paged
          contentType: text/plain
          body: member 2
        - dataResource: m3
          in: paged
          contentType: text/plain
          body: member 3
        - dataResource: m4
          in: paged
          contentType: text/plain
          body: member 4
        - dataResource: m5
          in: paged
          contentType: text/plain
          body: member 5
    steps:
      - label: the server splits a five-member container into pages (otherwise this test does not apply)
        precondition: true
        request:
          method: GET
          url: "${paged}"
          accept: application/lws+json
        response:
          statusCode: 200
          linkHeaders:
            - rel: first
            - rel: next
      - label: the first page names the last one (otherwise this test does not apply)
        precondition: true
        request:
          method: GET
          url: "${paged}"
          accept: application/lws+json
        response:
          statusCode: 200
          linkHeaders:
            - rel: last
              capture: last
      - label: the last page links to the first and to no next page
        request:
          method: GET
          url: "${last}"
          accept: application/lws+json
        response:
          statusCode: 200
          linkHeaders:
            - rel: first
            - rel: next
              absent: true

  - id: "#pagination-totalitems-all-pages"
    type: ValidationTest
    name: pagination-totalitems-all-pages
    label: totalItems counts the whole container on every page
    comment: >-
      "The totalItems property in the response body SHOULD reflect the total number of
      items across all pages, not just the current page." Five members: totalItems is 5
      on the first page and on the next.
    status: Proposed
    level: SHOULD
    source:
      - https://www.w3.org/TR/2026/WD-lws10-core-20260921/#requesting-pages
      - https://www.w3.org/TR/2026/WD-lws10-core-20260921/#pagination-model
    traits: [Get, Container]
    requirements:
      - https://example.org/touchstone/req/lws10-core/pagination-200-totalitems-all-pages
    prereqs:
      hierarchy:
        - container: paged
        - dataResource: m1
          in: paged
          contentType: text/plain
          body: member 1
        - dataResource: m2
          in: paged
          contentType: text/plain
          body: member 2
        - dataResource: m3
          in: paged
          contentType: text/plain
          body: member 3
        - dataResource: m4
          in: paged
          contentType: text/plain
          body: member 4
        - dataResource: m5
          in: paged
          contentType: text/plain
          body: member 5
    steps:
      - label: the server splits a five-member container into pages (otherwise this test does not apply)
        precondition: true
        request:
          method: GET
          url: "${paged}"
          accept: application/lws+json
        response:
          statusCode: 200
          linkHeaders:
            - rel: first
            - rel: next
      - label: the first page counts all five members
        request:
          method: GET
          url: "${paged}"
          accept: application/lws+json
        response:
          statusCode: 200
          linkHeaders:
            - rel: next
              capture: next
          json:
            - pointer: /totalItems
              equals: 5
      - label: and so does the next page
        request:
          method: GET
          url: "${next}"
          accept: application/lws+json
        response:
          statusCode: 200
          json:
            - pointer: /totalItems
              equals: 5
